{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FkXydNHD7hULSE1oC4i8QB","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_17/projects/recovery_aware_struct_prune/writing_plan.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"meta:\\\\n  venue: EMNLP 2026\\\\n  track: main\\\\n  template: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n  template_note: \\\\\\\"ARR May 25 submission. ACL template already set up in docs/paper/. Complete rewrite with new narrative (D045).\\\\\\\"\\\\n  page_limit: 8\\\\n  deadline: 2026-05-25\\\\n  core_claim: \\\\\\\"GRPO reward-accuracy decoupling is universal post-SFT; pruning additionally causes GRPO training collapse whose severity diagnoses structural damage type, revealing a Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff where structures optimal for SFT recovery are exactly those needed for RLVR stability.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  created_at: 2026-05-16T06:00:00Z\\\\n  updated_at: 2026-05-16T06:00:00Z\\\\n  framing_version: D045\\\\n  previous_narrative: \\\\\\\"Redundancy Paradox (RASP as universally superior) — invalidated by GRPO failure evidence\\\\\\\"\\\\n  narrative_shift: \\\\\\\"RASP reframed from 'best pruning method' to 'diagnostic tool' revealing fundamental pruning-alignment interaction\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\npaper_outline:\\\\n  - section: abstract\\\\n    word_budget: 200\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Problem: The prune-recover-align pipeline for reasoning LLMs assumes alignment is independent of pruning decisions\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Finding 1: GRPO reward improvement never translates to accuracy gain post-SFT (universal decoupling: unpruned 81.27%→81.27%, reward 0→0.661)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Finding 2: Pruning causes GRPO training collapse; severity gradient RASP (step 10, permanent) > Standard (step 60, transient) > Unpruned (never)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Finding 3: Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff — CKA-aware pruning benefits SFT (+2.2pp) but accelerates RLVR collapse 6×\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Implication: Optimal pruning is fundamentally conditioned on recovery paradigm\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments, method]\\\\n    tables: []\\\\n    figures: []\\\\n    registry_refs: [eval_grpo_unpruned_v2_step100, eval_sft_unpruned_full, eval_grpo_standard_step50_full, eval_grpo_rasp_v9_step5]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: introduction\\\\n    word_budget: 1200\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Hook: GRPO on unpruned DeepSeek-R1-7B drives reward from 0 to 0.661 over 100 steps — yet accuracy stays at exactly 81.27% (1072/1319 identical correct answers). Reward improvement is an illusion.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Twist: On pruned models, the illusion becomes catastrophic — GRPO training collapses entirely. CKA-aware pruning accelerates collapse 6× (step 10 vs step 60).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Gap: Current prune-recover-align pipeline treats stages as independent. Self-Healing (2025) reports 95% RLVR recovery — but doesn't document per-step dynamics or test structured pruning at moderate sparsity.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Insight: Functional redundancy plays opposite roles under SFT vs RLVR — removable for supervised redistribution, essential for RL exploration stability. This creates a Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"C1: GRPO reward-accuracy decoupling is universal; pruning adds training collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"C2: Collapse severity gradient diagnoses structural damage type (RASP 6× faster than Standard)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"C3: Recovery method conditions optimal pruning — RASP helps SFT (+2.2pp), harms RLVR (6× faster collapse)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments]\\\\n    citation_hints:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"selfhealing2025, rlsqueezes2026, shao2024grpo\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"graspprune2026, resp2025, deepseek2025r1\\\\\\\"\\\\n    figures: [fig1_framework]\\\\n    tables: []\\\\n    registry_refs: [eval_grpo_unpruned_v2_step100, eval_sft_unpruned_full, grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v9, grpo_stage2_standard_20_v8, grpo_unpruned_v2]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: related_work\\\\n    word_budget: 800\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cluster 1 — Structured Pruning for LLMs: LLM-Pruner→GRASPrune→RESP→Bonsai; none studies post-pruning alignment interaction\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cluster 2 — RLVR for Reasoning Models: GRPO, Self-Healing (claims 95% recovery), OPSD; none documents pruning-induced collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cluster 3 — Pruning-Recovery Interaction: Self-Healing (single ratio), PASER (data selection), PAT (joint); all treat pruning as black box for alignment\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cluster 4 — RL Training Instability: reward hacking literature, policy collapse in RLHF; not previously connected to structural pruning\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cluster 5 — CKA-based Methods: MPruner (inter-layer depth), Lachance (layer similarity); RASP uses intra-layer CKA as diagnostic\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [introduction]\\\\n    tables: []\\\\n    figures: []\\\\n    registry_refs: []\\\\n\\\\n  - section: method\\\\n    word_budget: 1500\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Framework overview: prune-recover-align pipeline + diagnostic methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"RASP scoring: S(h) = I(h) × (1-F(h))^α — importance × functional uniqueness\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Stage 1: Reasoning-trace structural analysis (Taylor importance + intra-layer CKA overlap)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Stage 2: Recovery-aware scoring (three regimes, per-layer capacity floor)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Stage 3: Two-stage recovery (SFT warmup → GRPO alignment)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Collapse diagnostic framework: collapse onset step, severity metrics (frac_zero_std, grad_norm, reward trajectory)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff: theoretical connection to RL Squeezes SFT Expands (ICLR 2026)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: []\\\\n    notation_table:\\\\n      \\\\\\\"I(h)\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Structural importance (Taylor expansion)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      \\\\\\\"F(h)\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Functional overlap (intra-layer CKA)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      \\\\\\\"S(h)\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recovery-aware score\\\\\\\"\\\\n      \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\alpha\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Overlap weight (default 1.0)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      \\\\\\\"t_c\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Collapse onset step\\\\\\\"\\\\n    figures: [fig1_framework]\\\\n    tables: []\\\\n    registry_refs: []\\\\n\\\\n  - section: experiments\\\\n    word_budget: 2500\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Setup: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, GSM8K (cal=512, eval=1319), 20% structured sparsity, 2×RTX PRO 6000\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Models: Unpruned, Standard (importance-only), RASP (α=1.0)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Recovery: SFT (2 epochs, lr=2e-5) → GRPO (100 steps, 8-bit AdamW, lr=1e-6, β=0.01, temp=0.4)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.1 GRPO Reward-Accuracy Decoupling (Claim 1): Table 1 + Figure 2. Universal across all 3 models.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.2 Collapse Dynamics (Claim 2): Figure 3 (training curves) + Table 2 (collapse metrics). Severity gradient.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.3 Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff (Claim 3): SFT comparison (RASP 61.49% vs Standard 59.29%) vs GRPO collapse comparison (RASP step 10 vs Standard step 60)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.4 α Ablation: Table 3. U-shape post-prune, CKA weight effect on collapse speed\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.5 Discussion: Connection to Self-Healing, practical recommendations\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: []\\\\n    registry_refs:\\\\n      - eval_grpo_unpruned_v2_step100\\\\n      - eval_grpo_unpruned_v2_step90\\\\n      - eval_sft_unpruned_full\\\\n      - eval_sft_standard_full_westd\\\\n      - eval_sft_rasp_full_westd\\\\n      - eval_grpo_standard_step50_full\\\\n      - eval_grpo_rasp_v9_step5\\\\n      - eval_grpo_rasp_v9_step10\\\\n      - grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v9\\\\n      - grpo_stage2_standard_20_v8\\\\n      - grpo_unpruned_v2\\\\n      - alpha_ablation_sweep\\\\n    tables: [tab1_main_results, tab2_collapse_dynamics, tab3_alpha_ablation]\\\\n    figures: [fig2_reward_accuracy, fig3_collapse_dynamics, fig4_cka_analysis]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: conclusion\\\\n    word_budget: 400\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Summary: First documentation of GRPO collapse on pruned reasoning models + Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Practical implication: SFT-only recovery is the safe path for structurally pruned models; RLVR alignment requires pruning-aware modifications\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Broader significance: Optimal pruning is paradigm-conditioned — no universal 'best structures to prune'\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Future: RLVR-compatible pruning methods, collapse-aware GRPO training, multi-model validation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments, method]\\\\n    tables: []\\\\n    figures: []\\\\n    registry_refs: []\\\\n\\\\nvisualization_plan:\\\\n  - fig_id: fig1_framework\\\\n    type: conceptual\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"RASP framework + diagnostic methodology. Left: Standard prune-recover-align pipeline (linear, treats stages as independent). Right: Our diagnostic approach — RASP as controlled perturbation revealing Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff. Show S(h) = I(h) × (1-F(h))^α scoring and its dual role (SFT benefit vs RLVR harm).\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: introduction\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"16:9\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"conceptual, no data needed\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig2_reward_accuracy\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Reward-Accuracy Decoupling. Left panel: GRPO reward over training steps (3 lines: Unpruned rises to 0.661, Standard peaks 0.096 then dips, RASP peaks 0.039 then collapses). Right panel: Bar chart of SFT Acc vs GRPO Acc for each model (showing Δ≈0 for all). Key message: reward improves, accuracy doesn't.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: grpo_unpruned_v2, grpo_stage2_standard_20_v8, grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v9, eval_grpo_unpruned_v2_step100, eval_grpo_standard_step50_full, eval_grpo_rasp_v9_step5\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"16:9\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig3_collapse_dynamics\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Collapse dynamics comparison. 2×2 subplot: (a) Reward over steps, (b) frac_zero_std over steps, (c) grad_norm over steps, (d) mean_terminated_length over steps. Three lines per subplot: Unpruned (stable), Standard (dip+recovery), RASP (permanent collapse). Annotate collapse onset steps. Data from W&B run logs.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"wandb: hwucjf2n (unpruned), q34vz8j2 (standard v8), mrikafz3 (rasp v9)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"4:3\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig4_cka_analysis\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"CKA overlap analysis supporting the tradeoff mechanism. (a) Per-layer mean CKA overlap bar chart (MLP vs Attention). (b) Structures pruned by RASP vs Standard — show RASP preferentially removes high-overlap groups while Standard is overlap-blind. Links CKA-based selection to collapse acceleration.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"existing CKA figures on remote (westb-23072) + pruning decision logs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"4:3\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\ntable_plan:\\\\n  - table_id: tab1_main_results\\\\n    caption: \\\\\\\"GRPO reward-accuracy decoupling across pruning conditions. N=1319 GSM8K, greedy decoding.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    type: comparison\\\\n    columns: [Model, SFT Acc (%), GRPO Acc (%), Δ (pp), Peak Reward, Collapse]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: eval_sft_unpruned_full, eval_grpo_unpruned_v2_step100, eval_sft_standard_full_westd, eval_grpo_standard_step50_full, eval_sft_rasp_full_westd, eval_grpo_rasp_v9_step5, grpo_unpruned_v2, grpo_stage2_standard_20_v8, grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v9\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Headline table. Δ = GRPO_Acc - SFT_Acc. All three show Δ ≤ 0. Unpruned Δ=0.00 is strongest evidence.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab2_collapse_dynamics\\\\n    caption: \\\\\\\"GRPO training collapse metrics by pruning method.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    type: comparison\\\\n    columns: [Model, Collapse Onset, Peak Reward, Final frac_zero_std, Final grad_norm, Recovery?]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: grpo_unpruned_v2, grpo_stage2_standard_20_v8, grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v9\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Severity gradient: RASP > Standard > Unpruned. 'Recovery?' column: Standard yes (transient), RASP no (permanent), Unpruned N/A.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab3_alpha_ablation\\\\n    caption: \\\\\\\"Effect of CKA overlap weight α on post-prune accuracy (N=100, 20% sparsity).\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    type: ablation\\\\n    columns: [\\\\\\\"α\\\\\\\", Post-prune Acc (%), EOS Rate, Avg Response Length]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: alpha_ablation_sweep\\\\\\\"\\\\n    baseline_row: \\\\\\\"α=0.0 (Standard equivalent)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"U-shape: α≥1.0 better post-prune. But higher α = more aggressive redundancy removal = faster GRPO collapse.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab4_hyperparams\\\\n    caption: \\\\\\\"Training hyperparameters for SFT warmup and GRPO alignment.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: appendix\\\\n    type: configuration\\\\n    columns: [Parameter, SFT Value, GRPO Value]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"manual\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nliterature_strategy:\\\\n  citation_budget: 35\\\\n  macro_context:\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"structured pruning for reasoning LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"RLVR alignment failure and reward hacking\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"pruning-recovery interaction\\\\\\\"\\\\n  micro_clusters:\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"Structured Pruning for LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"structured pruning large language model 2024 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"LLM-Pruner width pruning transformer\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"LLM-Pruner (Ma et al. 2023)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"GRASPrune (ACL 2026)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"RESP (Dec 2025)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Bonsai (2025)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 8\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"RLVR and Reasoning Recovery\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"reinforcement learning verifiable rewards reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"GRPO group relative policy optimization\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"GRPO (Shao et al. 2024)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Self-Healing (2025)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"DeepSeek-R1 (2025)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"RL Squeezes SFT Expands (ICLR 2026)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 7\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"Pruning-Recovery Interaction\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"pruning aware finetuning recovery LLM\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"compression alignment interaction\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"PASER (2025)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 5\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"RL Training Instability\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"reward hacking reinforcement learning language model\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"policy collapse RLHF training\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite: []\\\\n      target_count: 5\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"CKA-based Analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"centered kernel alignment neural network similarity\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"CKA pruning layer similarity\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Kornblith et al. 2019 (CKA)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"MPruner (2024)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 5\\\\n\\\\nwriting_order:\\\\n  - experiments\\\\n  - method\\\\n  - introduction\\\\n  - related_work\\\\n  - abstract\\\\n  - conclusion\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 13246, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019gBJmxYgZGGNCrMCzaXUAT","session_id":"9d5d0222-b1c1-4be8-b4de-29f6a4e65a4a","uuid":"fa5b61eb-9dc3-4228-9ff9-2905013dc300","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:35:10.105Z"}
