{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AzPjWrGB6ZY3DZS1RJ7suY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experiments}\n2\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{Experimental Setup}\n5\t\\label{sec:setup}\n6\t\n7\t\\paragraph{Model and data.}\n8\tWe use DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B~\\citep{deepseek2025r1}, a 7B-parameter reasoning model with Grouped Query Attention (28 query heads, 4 KV groups per layer, $G{=}7$) and FFN intermediate dimension 18944.\n9\tAll experiments use GSM8K~\\citep{cobbe2021gsm8k}: 512 training examples for calibration, 1319 test problems for evaluation with greedy decoding and exact-match accuracy.\n10\t\n11\t\\paragraph{Pruning methods.}\n12\tWe compare three conditions at 20\\% structured sparsity:\n13\t(1)~\\textbf{Unpruned}: the original model (upper bound);\n14\t(2)~\\textbf{Standard}: importance-only pruning via Taylor expansion $I(h) = |w_h \\cdot g_h|$, ranking and removing the lowest-scoring structures globally;\n15\t(3)~\\textbf{RASP}: recovery-aware scoring $S(h) = I(h) \\times (1-F(h))^\\alpha$ with $\\alpha{=}1.0$, where $F(h)$ is the intra-layer CKA functional overlap.\n16\tAt 20\\% sparsity, all 200 pruned structures are MLP channel groups (0/112 attention GQA groups are pruned under any method), as attention groups have uniformly higher importance.\n17\tA per-layer capacity floor ensures ${\\geq}40\\%$ MLP retention per layer.\n18\t\n19\t\\paragraph{Recovery pipeline.}\n20\tAll pruned models undergo identical two-stage recovery:\n21\t\\emph{SFT warmup} (2 epochs, lr=$2{\\times}10^{-5}$, effective batch size 8, GSM8K training data) to restore EOS generation and format compliance, followed by \\emph{GRPO alignment}~\\citep{shao2024grpo} (8-bit AdamW, lr=$1{\\times}10^{-6}$, $\\beta_\\text{KL}{=}0.01$, temperature 0.4, repetition penalty 1.15, format reward $+0.1$ for \\texttt{\\textbackslash boxed\\{\\}}, max completion length 1024, 100 steps) on 2$\\times$NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 (96GB).\n22\tThe unpruned model undergoes the same SFT$\\to$GRPO pipeline for controlled comparison.\n23\t\n24\t\\subsection{GRPO Reward-Accuracy Decoupling}\n25\t\\label{sec:decoupling}\n26\t\n27\tTable~\\ref{tab:main} presents our central finding: \\textbf{GRPO reward improvement does not translate to accuracy gain for any model post-SFT.}\n28\t\n29\t\\begin{table}[t]\n30\t\\centering\n31\t\\caption{GRPO reward-accuracy decoupling. All accuracy values: $N{=}1319$, GSM8K, greedy, seed=42. GRPO Acc is measured at the best available checkpoint before or at collapse onset.}\n32\t\\label{tab:main}\n33\t\\small\n34\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccl@{}}\n35\t\\toprule\n36\tModel & SFT Acc & GRPO Acc & $\\Delta$ & Peak Reward & Collapse \\\\\n37\t\\midrule\n38\tUnpruned & 81.27 & 81.27 & 0.00 & 0.661 & None \\\\\n39\tStandard 20\\% & 59.29 & 58.91 & $-$0.38 & 0.096 & Transient \\\\\n40\tRASP 20\\% & 61.49 & 61.87 & $+$0.38 & 0.039 & Permanent \\\\\n41\t\\bottomrule\n42\t\\end{tabular}\n43\t\\end{table}\n44\t\n45\tThe unpruned model provides the strongest evidence: over 100 GRPO steps, reward rises from 0 to 0.661---a 100-fold increase---yet accuracy remains at exactly 81.27\\%, with the \\emph{identical} 1072 out of 1319 test problems answered correctly before and after GRPO.\n46\tThis is not a failure to detect small improvements: the pre- and post-GRPO models produce the same set of correct answers.\n47\tGRPO's reward signal, which measures whether sampled completions reach the correct answer during training, does not encode improvements in greedy-decoded reasoning chains.\n48\t\n49\tFor pruned models, the pattern holds: Standard shows $-0.38$pp and RASP shows $+0.38$pp, both statistically indistinguishable from their SFT baselines ($p{=}0.52$ for RASP, two-proportion $z$-test).\n50\tThe reward signal is correspondingly weaker (peak 0.096 and 0.039 vs.\\ 0.661 for unpruned), and even differs qualitatively---RASP undergoes complete training collapse (\\S\\ref{sec:collapse})---yet accuracy remains unchanged.\n51\tThis reveals \\emph{bidirectional} reward-accuracy decoupling: neither reward \\emph{increase} (unpruned: $+0.661$ reward, $\\Delta{=}0$pp accuracy) nor reward \\emph{collapse} (RASP: reward drops to 0.015, $\\Delta{=}{+}0.38$pp accuracy) alters reasoning performance.\n52\tGRPO reward and GSM8K accuracy are orthogonal under our experimental conditions.\n53\t\n54\t\\subsection{Collapse Dynamics and Severity Gradient}\n55\t\\label{sec:collapse}\n56\t\n57\tWhile reward-accuracy decoupling is universal, pruning introduces a qualitatively distinct pathology: \\textbf{GRPO training collapse}---the complete failure of the RL training process, characterized by vanishing gradients, degenerate sampling, and cessation of meaningful policy updates.\n58\tThe three models exhibit a clean severity gradient that diagnoses structural damage type.\n59\t\n60\t\\paragraph{Collapse metrics.}\n61\tWe track four diagnostic signals at each training step: (1)~mean reward across rollouts, (2)~\\texttt{frac\\_zero\\_std}---the fraction of prompts where all rollout rewards are identical (indicating no exploration signal), (3)~gradient norm, and (4)~mean terminated completion length.\n62\tTable~\\ref{tab:collapse} summarizes the collapse trajectory for each model.\n63\t\n64\t\\begin{table}[t]\n65\t\\centering\n66\t\\caption{GRPO training collapse metrics by pruning method. Collapse onset = first step where $\\texttt{frac\\_zero\\_std} \\geq 0.85$ or $\\texttt{grad\\_norm} < 0.05$.}\n67\t\\label{tab:collapse}\n68\t\\small\n69\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\n70\t\\toprule\n71\tModel & Onset & Peak Reward & Final $f_\\text{zero}$ & Final $\\|\\nabla\\|$ & Recovery \\\\\n72\t\\midrule\n73\tUnpruned & --- & 0.669 & $\\leq$0.275 & 6.97 & N/A \\\\\n74\tStandard & Step 60 & 0.096 & 0.475 & 2.14 & Yes \\\\\n75\tRASP & Step 10 & 0.039 & 0.950 & 0.007 & No \\\\\n76\t\\bottomrule\n77\t\\end{tabular}\n78\t\\end{table}\n79\t\n80\t\\paragraph{Unpruned: stable training.}\n81\tThe unpruned model trains stably for all 100 steps.\n82\tReward rises monotonically from 0 to 0.661 (peak 0.669 at step 80), gradient norms remain healthy (2.45--14.94), KL divergence is tightly bounded (0.0046--0.0053), and \\texttt{frac\\_zero\\_std} never exceeds 0.275.\n83\tA shallow reward dip at step 30 (0.425) recovers by step 40.\n84\tThis confirms that GRPO itself is functional on intact architectures---collapse is pruning-specific.\n85\t\n86\t\\paragraph{Standard pruning: transient instability.}\n87\tStandard-pruned models follow a three-phase trajectory: (1)~initial learning (steps 1--40, reward peaks at 0.096, \\texttt{frac\\_zero\\_std}$\\approx$0.45, healthy gradients at 3.45); (2)~transient collapse at step 60 (gradient norm drops to 0.013, \\texttt{frac\\_zero\\_std} rises to 0.525); (3)~recovery by step 90 (gradient norm returns to 2.14, reward stabilizes at 0.065).\n88\tThe step-50 checkpoint evaluation ($N{=}1319$) yields 58.91\\%---0.38pp below SFT---indicating that even the pre-collapse training phase failed to improve reasoning accuracy.\n89\t\n90\t\\paragraph{RASP pruning: permanent collapse.}\n91\tRASP-pruned models collapse catastrophically within 10 steps and never recover.\n92\tBy step 10, \\texttt{frac\\_zero\\_std} reaches 0.875 and reward is 0.015; by step 30, \\texttt{frac\\_zero\\_std} rises to 0.95, clipped ratio hits 0.956 (nearly all completions truncated at max length), and the effective gradient signal has vanished.\n93\tCrucially, this training collapse does \\emph{not} degrade reasoning accuracy.\n94\tEvaluations at step 5 (60.65\\%), step 10 (61.87\\%), and the SFT baseline (61.49\\%) are statistically indistinguishable ($N{=}1319$, $p{=}0.52$, two-proportion $z$-test).\n95\tThe collapse destroys GRPO's training dynamics---exploration diversity, gradient signal, and reward optimization---but leaves the model's greedy-decoded reasoning capability intact, consistent with the bidirectional decoupling observed in \\S\\ref{sec:decoupling}.\n96\t\n97\t\\paragraph{Severity gradient interpretation.}\n98\tThe 6$\\times$ acceleration from Standard (step 60) to RASP (step 10) is directly attributable to their pruning criteria.\n99\tRASP preferentially removes high-overlap (functionally redundant) structures---those whose computation is shared with within-layer peers.\n100\tWe hypothesize that these redundant structures serve as \\emph{exploration buffers} during RL training: backup computational paths that allow the policy to explore without catastrophic parameter updates.\n101\tStandard pruning, which is overlap-blind, retains some redundant structures by chance, preserving partial exploration capacity and delaying collapse.\n102\tImportantly, the collapse severity gradient diagnoses \\emph{training process} damage, not reasoning capability damage: both Standard and RASP maintain their SFT-level accuracy through and after collapse (\\S\\ref{sec:decoupling}).\n103\tThe gradient thus serves as a zero-cost diagnostic of structural compatibility with RL optimization, independent of the model's task performance.\n104\t\n105\t\\subsection{Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff}\n106\t\\label{sec:tradeoff}\n107\t\n108\tThe preceding sections reveal that CKA-aware pruning has \\emph{opposite effects} depending on the recovery paradigm:\n109\t\n110\t\\paragraph{SFT recovery: RASP advantage.}\n111\tUnder SFT, RASP-pruned models recover to 61.49\\% versus 59.29\\% for Standard---a +2.2pp advantage ($N{=}1319$, $p{=}0.25$ by two-proportion $z$-test, not statistically significant at this sample size).\n112\tThe direction is consistent with the original RASP design rationale: removing redundant structures whose function can be redistributed through gradient-based learning, while protecting unique structures that SFT cannot recreate.\n113\t\n114\t\\paragraph{RLVR stability: RASP harmful.}\n115\tUnder GRPO, the same CKA-aware pruning that marginally benefits SFT causes 6$\\times$ faster training collapse (step 10 vs.\\ step 60).\n116\tWhile this collapse does not affect greedy-decoded accuracy (\\S\\ref{sec:collapse}), it renders GRPO training non-functional: gradient signals vanish, exploration diversity collapses, and the reward optimization loop ceases to operate.\n117\tFunctional redundancy, which is expendable for supervised redistribution, provides critical exploration stability for RL---backup paths that prevent policy collapse during reward-driven optimization.\n118\t\n119\t\\paragraph{The tradeoff.}\n120\tThis creates a fundamental \\textbf{Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff}: the structures optimal to prune for SFT recovery (high-overlap, functionally redundant) are exactly those needed for RLVR training stability.\n121\tNo single pruning strategy can simultaneously optimize for both recovery paradigms.\n122\tThis is consistent with the ``RL Squeezes, SFT Expands'' finding~\\citep{rlsqueezes2026}---RLVR recovers through global exploration (requiring structural diversity for stable policy search), while SFT recovers through local trajectory imitation (requiring only that the target function is representable).\n123\t\n124\t\\subsection{Ablation: CKA Overlap Weight}\n125\t\\label{sec:ablation}\n126\t\n127\tWe vary the overlap penalty weight $\\alpha \\in \\{0, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0\\}$ in the RASP scoring formula $S(h) = I(h) \\times (1-F(h))^\\alpha$, where $\\alpha{=}0$ reduces to importance-only (Standard-equivalent) pruning.\n128\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation} reports post-prune accuracy ($N{=}100$, 512 calibration examples).\n129\t\n130\t\\begin{table}[t]\n131\t\\centering\n132\t\\caption{Post-prune GSM8K accuracy by CKA weight $\\alpha$ ($N{=}100$, 20\\% sparsity).}\n133\t\\label{tab:ablation}\n134\t\\small\n135\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}cccS[table-format=4.0]@{}}\n136\t\\toprule\n137\t$\\alpha$ & Acc (\\%) & EOS Rate & {Avg Len} \\\\\n138\t\\midrule\n139\t0.0 & 27 & 0.80 & 1192 \\\\\n140\t0.25 & 23 & 0.63 & 1834 \\\\\n141\t0.5 & 22 & 0.81 & 1141 \\\\\n142\t1.0 & 31 & 0.92 & 733 \\\\\n143\t2.0 & 36 & 0.59 & 2130 \\\\\n144\t\\bottomrule\n145\t\\end{tabular}\n146\t\\end{table}\n147\t\n148\tThe relationship between $\\alpha$ and post-prune accuracy is non-monotonic (U-shaped): moderate overlap penalty ($\\alpha{=}0.25$--$0.5$) performs worse than either extreme.\n149\tAt $\\alpha{\\geq}1.0$, stronger CKA weighting improves immediate post-prune reasoning (31--36\\% vs.\\ 22--27\\% for low $\\alpha$), confirming that overlap-aware group selection preserves reasoning-critical structures.\n150\tHowever, this improved post-prune accuracy comes at the cost of faster GRPO collapse---higher $\\alpha$ removes more redundant structures, reducing the exploration buffer available for RL training.\n151\tThe U-shape suggests a phase transition in group selection: below a threshold overlap weight, the CKA signal introduces noise relative to importance-only ranking; above it, overlap-based reranking consistently selects more reasoning-preserving (but RL-destabilizing) pruning configurations.\n152\t\n153\t\\subsection{From Diagnosis to Solution: RASP-RLVR}\n154\t\\label{sec:rasp_rlvr_exp}\n155\t\n156\tThe Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff predicts that \\emph{inverting} the CKA signal---protecting rather than removing functionally redundant structures---should stabilize GRPO training.\n157\tWe test this prediction with \\rasp{}-RLVR ($S_\\text{RLVR}(h) = I(h) \\times F(h)^\\alpha$; \\S\\ref{sec:rasp_rlvr}), which preferentially prunes structures with \\emph{low} overlap (functionally unique) while preserving high-overlap exploration buffers.\n158\t\n159\t\\paragraph{Collapse dynamics comparison.}\n160\tTable~\\ref{tab:rlvr_collapse} compares GRPO training stability across four pruning conditions at 20\\% sparsity.\n161\t\n162\t\\begin{table}[t]\n163\t\\centering\n164\t\\caption{GRPO training collapse: RASP-RLVR vs.\\ baselines (20\\% sparsity, $N{=}1319$).}\n165\t\\label{tab:rlvr_collapse}\n166\t\\small\n167\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\n168\t\\toprule\n169\tMethod & Onset & Peak Reward & Final $f_\\text{zero}$ & Final $\\|\\nabla\\|$ & GRPO Acc \\\\\n170\t\\midrule\n171\tUnpruned & --- & 0.669 & $\\leq$0.275 & 6.97 & 81.27 \\\\\n172\tRASP-RLVR & [TBD-RLVR] & [TBD-RLVR] & [TBD-RLVR] & [TBD-RLVR] & [TBD-RLVR] \\\\\n173\tStandard & Step 60 & 0.096 & 0.475 & 2.14 & 58.91 \\\\\n174\tRASP & Step 10 & 0.039 & 0.950 & 0.007 & 61.87 \\\\\n175\t\\bottomrule\n176\t\\end{tabular}\n177\t\\end{table}\n178\t\n179\t[TBD-RLVR: Analysis of whether RASP-RLVR delays/prevents collapse relative to Standard and RASP. Key questions: (1) Does preserving redundancy delay collapse onset? (2) If collapse is delayed, does extended GRPO training translate to accuracy improvement---or does reward-accuracy decoupling persist? (3) What is the SFT accuracy cost of pruning functionally unique structures?]\n180\t\n181\t\\paragraph{SFT-RLVR tradeoff.}\n182\t[TBD-RLVR: RASP-RLVR's SFT accuracy vs.\\ RASP's SFT accuracy. If RASP-RLVR sacrifices SFT performance to gain RLVR stability, this directly validates the Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff as actionable: practitioners choose pruning criterion based on intended recovery paradigm.]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019gBJmxYgZGGNCrMCzaXUAT","session_id":"9d5d0222-b1c1-4be8-b4de-29f6a4e65a4a","uuid":"36557fd5-dc19-4849-8e05-96b35d5bd748","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:35:38.983Z"}
