{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014HRJk3MtU6cu5dvDWdeSpU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{GRPO Alignment Fails on Structurally Pruned Reasoning Models:\\\\Reward Hacking, Training Collapse, and the Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{Anonymous}\n16\t\n17\t\\begin{document}\n18\t\\maketitle\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{abstract}\n21\tWe present the first systematic study of how structural pruning interacts with RLVR (Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards) alignment in reasoning LLMs. We discover that GRPO alignment universally fails to improve reasoning accuracy---even on unpruned models, reward increases from 0 to 0.661 while accuracy remains unchanged (81.27\\% $\\rightarrow$ 81.27\\%, identical 1072/1319 correct answers). On structurally pruned models, GRPO additionally induces \\emph{training collapse}: gradient norms drop to near-zero and policy diversity vanishes. To diagnose this phenomenon, we introduce RASP (Redundancy-Aware Structured Pruning), a CKA-based scoring method that reveals a fundamental \\emph{Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff}: structures that are safely removable under SFT recovery (functionally redundant pathways) provide critical exploration stability for RLVR. RASP-pruned models collapse 6$\\times$ faster than standard importance-pruned models (step 10 vs.\\ step 60), while unstructured pruning produces no collapse at all---confirming that training collapse is a structural damage phenomenon. We validate across two models, four pruning methods, two sparsity levels, and three recovery paradigms, establishing that the prune-recover-align pipeline requires fundamental reconsideration when RLVR alignment is intended.\n22\t\\end{abstract}\n23\t\n24\t\\section{Introduction}\n25\t\n26\tThe deployment pipeline for compressed reasoning LLMs typically follows a three-stage process: structural pruning to reduce model size, supervised fine-tuning (SFT) to recover lost capabilities, and reinforcement learning alignment (RLVR) to further improve reasoning~\\cite{self-healing-2025}. This pipeline implicitly assumes that pruning decisions are independent of the downstream alignment method---that structures safe to remove for SFT recovery are equally safe for RLVR alignment.\n27\t\n28\tWe demonstrate that this assumption fails catastrophically. Through controlled experiments on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, we establish three findings:\n29\t\n30\t\\paragraph{Finding 1: GRPO reward-accuracy decoupling is universal.} GRPO does not improve reasoning accuracy for \\emph{any} model configuration post-SFT. On the unpruned model, 100 steps of GRPO training increase reward from 0 to 0.661 while accuracy remains at exactly 81.27\\% (1072/1319 correct on GSM8K). On pruned models, GRPO additionally induces complete training collapse---policy diversity vanishes and gradient norms approach zero.\n31\t\n32\t\\paragraph{Finding 2: Collapse dynamics diagnose structural damage.} The speed of GRPO collapse varies systematically with pruning method: RASP (which removes functionally redundant structures) collapses at step $\\sim$10, GRASPrune at step $\\sim$18, Bonsai at step $\\sim$28, and standard importance pruning at step $\\sim$60. This ordering directly reflects how aggressively each method removes structural redundancy, establishing collapse speed as a zero-cost diagnostic of structural damage type.\n33\t\n34\t\\paragraph{Finding 3: Optimal pruning depends on recovery paradigm.} Under SFT recovery, RASP shows a consistent marginal advantage (+2.2pp at 20\\% sparsity, +3.2pp at 25\\%). Under RLVR, RASP is actively harmful---it collapses 6$\\times$ faster than standard pruning. This Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff means the structures optimal to prune for SFT are exactly those needed for RLVR stability.\n35\t\n36\tCrucially, unstructured pruning at the same sparsity (20\\%) produces \\emph{no} training collapse---GRPO trains stably for 100 steps with reward reaching 0.548. This confirms that collapse is a \\emph{structural} damage phenomenon: removing entire computational units eliminates exploration pathways that RLVR requires, while zeroing individual weights preserves network topology and exploration capacity.\n37\t\n38\tOur results challenge Self-Healing~\\cite{self-healing-2025}, which claims RLVR recovers pruned reasoning models. We show that under structured pruning with controlled accuracy evaluation, GRPO produces reward improvement without accuracy gain followed by training collapse. The prune-recover-align pipeline requires fundamental reconsideration when RLVR alignment is the target.\n39\t\n40\t\\section{Related Work}\n41\t\n42\t\\paragraph{Structural pruning for LLMs.} LLM-Pruner~\\cite{llmpruner-2023} introduced dependency-aware structural pruning for transformer models. SliceGPT~\\cite{slicegpt-2024} prunes via rotation-invariant projections. GRASPrune~\\cite{grasprune-2026} achieves SOTA budgeted structural pruning via learned gates. Bonsai~\\cite{bonsai-2025} uses activation norms for gradient-free pruning. These methods evaluate pruning quality via post-prune or post-SFT accuracy without studying RLVR alignment interaction.\n43\t\n44\t\\paragraph{Recovery and alignment of pruned models.} Self-Healing~\\cite{self-healing-2025} claims RLVR (specifically GRPO) can recover pruned reasoning models to 95\\%+ of original accuracy at 25\\% unstructured sparsity. PASER~\\cite{paser-2025} optimizes recovery data selection. RESP~\\cite{resp-2025} uses reasoning-trace calibration. None document GRPO training collapse on structurally pruned models or study the interaction between pruning strategy and alignment stability.\n45\t\n46\t\\paragraph{CKA in neural network analysis.} Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA)~\\cite{cka-2019} measures representational similarity between neural network layers. MPruner~\\cite{mpruner-2024} uses inter-layer CKA for depth pruning in CNNs. We use intra-layer CKA as a \\emph{functional redundancy} metric that reveals the Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff in reasoning LLMs.\n47\t\n48\t\\paragraph{RLVR for reasoning.} GRPO~\\cite{grpo-2024} provides group-relative policy optimization for reasoning tasks. Recent work on reasoning LLM alignment~\\cite{deepseek-r1-2024} uses GRPO with verifiable rewards. The ``RL Squeezes, SFT Expands'' hypothesis~\\cite{rl-squeezes-2026} proposes that RL narrows representation diversity while SFT broadens it---consistent with our observation that RL requires structural redundancy for stable exploration.\n49\t\n50\t\\section{Method}\n51\t\n52\t\\subsection{RASP: Redundancy-Aware Structured Pruning}\n53\t\n54\tWe introduce RASP as a diagnostic pruning method designed to reveal the role of functional redundancy in alignment stability. For each prunable structure $h$ (attention head or MLP group), RASP computes:\n55\t\\begin{equation}\n56\tS(h) = I(h) \\times (1 - F(h))^{\\alpha}\n57\t\\label{eq:rasp}\n58\t\\end{equation}\n59\twhere $I(h)$ is the standard importance score (gradient $\\times$ magnitude) and $F(h) \\in [0,1]$ is the functional redundancy score based on CKA similarity with other structures in the same layer:\n60\t\\begin{equation}\n61\tF(h) = \\max_{h' \\neq h, h' \\in \\text{layer}(h)} \\text{CKA}(\\mathbf{X}_h, \\mathbf{X}_{h'})\n62\t\\end{equation}\n63\tHere $\\mathbf{X}_h \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times d}$ is the activation matrix of structure $h$ over $n$ calibration samples. The penalty $(1-F(h))^\\alpha$ reduces the score of structures with \\emph{low} redundancy (unique, non-replaceable structures), causing RASP to preferentially prune structures with high functional overlap.\n64\t\n65\tThe hyperparameter $\\alpha$ controls the strength of the redundancy penalty. At $\\alpha=0$, RASP reduces to standard importance pruning.\n66\t\n67\t\\subsection{Pruning Pipeline}\n68\t\n69\tWe prune at the granularity of attention heads and MLP intermediate groups. Given a target sparsity ratio $s$, we rank all structures by $S(h)$ and remove the bottom-$s$ fraction. No iterative or progressive pruning is used---all structures are removed in a single pass.\n70\t\n71\t\\subsection{Recovery Protocols}\n72\t\n73\t\\paragraph{SFT Recovery.} Supervised fine-tuning on GSM8K training set with teacher forcing. Training for 2000 steps with learning rate 2e-5 and batch size 8.\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{GRPO Recovery.} Group Relative Policy Optimization on GSM8K with verifiable answer checking. 100 training steps, group size 8, learning rate 5e-7, KL coefficient 0.04. We monitor collapse indicators: fraction of zero-variance groups (policy diversity), gradient norm, and reward trajectory.\n76\t\n77\t\\subsection{Collapse Indicators}\n78\t\n79\tWe define GRPO training collapse via three simultaneous conditions:\n80\t\\begin{enumerate}\n81\t\\item \\textbf{Policy diversity loss}: fraction of zero-standard-deviation response groups exceeds 0.85\n82\t\\item \\textbf{Gradient vanishing}: gradient norm drops below 0.01\n83\t\\item \\textbf{Irreversibility}: conditions persist for $\\geq$5 consecutive steps\n84\t\\end{enumerate}\n85\tThe \\emph{collapse step} is the first step where all three conditions are met.\n86\t\n87\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n88\t\n89\t\\paragraph{Models.} Primary: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (7B parameters, distilled reasoning model). Cross-model validation: Qwen2.5-Math-7B (math-specialized).\n90\t\n91\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.} GSM8K test set, $N=1319$ problems, greedy decoding. We report accuracy (fraction correct) with standard errors. Extended evaluation on MATH-500, HumanEval, and MMLU for out-of-distribution assessment.\n92\t\n93\t\\paragraph{Pruning methods compared.}\n94\t\\begin{itemize}\n95\t\\item \\textbf{RASP} ($\\alpha=1.0$): CKA-aware importance scoring (Eq.~\\ref{eq:rasp})\n96\t\\item \\textbf{Standard}: Importance-only ($\\alpha=0$, gradient $\\times$ magnitude)\n97\t\\item \\textbf{GRASPrune}: Gate-learned structural importance (ACL 2026 SOTA)\n98\t\\item \\textbf{Bonsai}: Activation-norm-based (gradient-free, ICML 2025)\n99\t\\item \\textbf{Unstructured}: Magnitude-based weight pruning (no structural removal)\n100\t\\end{itemize}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01N2LYAXsb5jvhZHwRrXqovf","session_id":"9d5d0222-b1c1-4be8-b4de-29f6a4e65a4a","uuid":"2b7b240e-35d9-4314-9b33-8bf82bb729db","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:36:16.186Z"}
